# mobsfscan project exemptions (round-68 T13, rationale updated round-69) —
#   see round68-audit.md §T13 and the round-69 audit §T-mobsf.
#
# android_logging (CWE-532): every Log.* call that interpolates a variable is
# flagged (264 findings on the full tree with mobsfscan 1.2.0). Project-wide
# documented stance (round-56 SEC-06, proguard-rules.pro:135-139): release
# builds strip Log.v/d/i/w via -assumenosideeffects; only Log.e survives,
# and E-level calls that embed file paths are exactly two documented
# residuals (StockfishNative engine-extraction failure paths, none of them
# user data). The app is offline-first (V3-38): no user data is ever logged.
#
# round-69 (CodeRabbit PR#57 Minor, root fix): the one android_logging
# finding that DID carry user data — StockfishNative.sendUciCommand's
# Log.e("Error sending command: " + command), whose command can embed a FEN
# (the full game position) — is FIXED AT THE ROOT (the command text is no
# longer logged; the message is static + command.length() only). That finding
# no longer exists to suppress. The suppression is nevertheless KEPT:
#   - the remaining ~263 findings are Log.v/d/i/w (release-stripped) plus the
#     two documented path-bearing Log.e residuals — none carry user data;
#   - mobsfscan has no per-line suppression granularity in .mobsf (only
#     ignore-rules / ignore-paths / ignore-filenames), so "delete the global
#     rule" would re-open ~263 SARIF alerts whose only effect is burying real
#     signals in noise (alert fatigue is itself a security anti-pattern);
#   - the release-strip stance is enforced by proguard and verified by the
#     round-56 review chain.
# If mobsfscan grows line-level suppression, prefer narrowing this rule to
# the two documented E-level residuals and re-reviewing the v/d/i/w class.
#
# Why repo-side instead of per-finding UI dismissal: GitHub code scanning
# dismissals are per-finding-instance — every new code line re-reports the
# class. This file is picked up automatically: the mobsf workflow scans
# `src/main/java` first, and mobsfscan looks for .mobsf in the first scanned
# path (verified empirically: 264 -> 0 findings, other rules unaffected).
#
# NOTE: dotfiles have failed to land through the zip overlay channel before
# (the .gitignore incident, round-67 T0). If the post-upload remote re-check
# shows this file missing, create it via the GitHub web editor with this
# exact content (see the round-68 delivery README).
ignore-rules:
  - android_logging
